# Copy to /opt/solinteg/solinteg.env and fill in. Loaded by both systemd units.
# chmod 600 — it holds secrets.
#
# IMPORTANT: this is a plain KEY=VALUE file read by systemd's EnvironmentFile= directive,
# which does NOT support inline "# comment" text after a value — it becomes part of the
# value verbatim (confirmed 2026-07-10 on-device: `X=98   # comment` is parsed as
# X="98   # comment", which a Python float()/int() cast on the other end crashes on). Every
# comment below is on its own line, never after a value, for exactly this reason — keep that
# convention if you add more.

# ── Inverter Modbus connection (poller + controller) ──
# 192.168.1.50 below is a placeholder for the simple case: inverter on your home LAN with a
# DHCP reservation. The reference deployment instead runs the inverter on its own isolated
# subnet (192.168.99.2) with no direct LAN exposure — Modbus TCP has no auth, so anything on
# the same network segment as the inverter can command it. See deploy/README.md §13 and
# deploy/network-isolation/ if you want that same isolation; it's optional but recommended.
# Set a DHCP reservation for the inverter's IP either way.
SOLINTEG_HOST=192.168.1.50
SOLINTEG_PORT=502
# Dongle forwards to RTU slave 255 (0xFF), NOT 1 — see MODBUS.md.
SOLINTEG_SLAVE_ID=255
POLL_INTERVAL=30

# ── Inverter control safety gates (see inverter_control.py / MODBUS.md) ──
# ARMED is deliberately NOT set here: every register write is a no-op until you
# explicitly export SOLINTEG_CONTROL_ARMED=1 for the process that should write.
#
# RESTART BOTH solinteg-dispatch AND solinteg-web when you change this. Both read this file
# only at process start. Today only the executor branches on the flag, so restarting the web
# app is precautionary — but the PLANNER starts branching on it the moment hold mode lands
# (it is gated to disarmed installs: no register expresses "don't charge, export the surplus",
# and dispatch_loop's apply_target maps 'hold' to return_to_auto, which stores the surplus —
# the exact inverse). From then on, restarting only the executor leaves the planner on the
# stale side, still emitting hold slots that a freshly-armed dispatch executes as charging,
# until something unrelated restarts the web app. The dashboard would show the plan it meant
# to run while the battery did the opposite, which is why this is worth doing before it can
# bite rather than after.
#SOLINTEG_CONTROL_ARMED=1
# Confirmed on-device 2026-07-02 by scripts/tools/probe_50207_sign.py.
SOLINTEG_50207_SIGN=neg_charge
# The five below are read by BOTH the Python control scripts AND the web app (lib/constants.ts)
# — one shared value per hardware fact, not per-language copies. Since 2026-07-10.
# Also written to inverter regs 52502/52503 (native protection).
SOLINTEG_SOC_FLOOR_PCT=8
# Economic minimum SoC (set 20 on Sundsvall). Defaults to the physical floor.
# Does not change the BMS/inverter hardware floor. Enforced by the DP optimizer.
#SOLINTEG_PLANNER_RESERVE_PCT=20
# Enforced in inverter_control.py; the DP optimizer doesn't model it (see lib/constants.ts).
SOLINTEG_SOC_CEILING_PCT=98
# Hardware charge/discharge limit — clamped before any write.
#SOLINTEG_BATTERY_MAX_W=15360
# Your fuse rating's power cap — the one most other installs differ on.
#SOLINTEG_GRID_CAP_W=11000
# Usable capacity, kWh.
#SOLINTEG_BATTERY_KWH=25.6
# Round-trip efficiency (datasheet value).
#SOLINTEG_BATTERY_RT_EFF=0.96
# Inverter conversion efficiency for ONE leg (AC->DC charging, DC->AC discharging). The optimizer's
# per-leg efficiency is sqrt(SOLINTEG_BATTERY_RT_EFF) x this, because the DP prices GRID energy in
# and out -- so it needs the AC-to-AC path, not just the cells'. Using the DC datasheet figure alone
# claimed a 96% AC round trip against a realistic 88-90%, understating the cost of a stored kWh by
# ~8 ore at a 90 ore buy price -- more than the wear cost, enough to flip marginal cycling the wrong
# way. Default 0.97 is this inverter family's datasheet class. Measure yours with
# scripts/tools/measure-battery-rt-eff.py, which now reports both figures. See OPTIMIZER-AUDIT.md P1-5.
#SOLINTEG_INVERTER_CONV_EFF=0.97

# ── Load forecasting robustness (web app only, since 2026-07-18 — see DESIGN-reserve.md §9) ──
# The DP plans against consumptionKwh × this margin so the trajectory keeps slack for
# load-forecast error instead of kissing the SoC floor (1 disables; logged forecasts stay honest).
#SOLINTEG_LOAD_FORECAST_MARGIN=1.15
# Trailing window (days) for the live per-hour load profile read from telemetry readings,
# replacing the static fitted hour shape when ≥5 days of data exist (0 disables).
#SOLINTEG_LIVE_LOAD_DAYS=14

# ── Risk-aware planning (web app only, since 2026-07-19 — see lib/constants.ts rationale) ──
# Deferral bias: öre/kWh per hour of earliness on grid-funded charges and battery-to-grid
# sells, so near-equal-price actions land as late as possible and replans can still cancel
# them (0 disables).
#SOLINTEG_DEFERRAL_RATE_ORE=0.4
# How far ahead the deferral bias still discriminates (hours). The surcharge is
# `rate x max(0, this - hoursFromNow)`, so its maximum level is rate x this -- 4.8 ore/kWh at the
# defaults, an order of magnitude below a real daily spread. It used to be measured from the
# horizon's END and unbounded, which meant a longer price horizon raised the surcharge on every
# grid-funded kWh (9.5 ore/kWh at one day, 28.7 at three) and taxed charging instead of just
# retiming it. See OPTIMIZER-AUDIT.md P1-2.
#SOLINTEG_DEFERRAL_HORIZON_H=12
# Hard cap (öre, whole-plan) on real value the deferral may give up before the plan falls
# back to the undeferred optimum.
# Cap (ore, whole plan) on the TRUE value ALL the risk knobs together may give up -- the deferral
# bias, the solar risk premium and the forecast price premium. The DP solves twice (all knobs off
# vs as configured), prices both at true prices, and falls back to the fearless plan beyond the cap.
# It covered the deferral bias alone until 2026-09-10, leaving the two larger knobs unmeasured.
# SOLINTEG_DEFERRAL_MAX_SACRIFICE_ORE is still read as a fallback for existing deployments.
#SOLINTEG_RISK_MAX_SACRIFICE_ORE=50
# Uncertainty premium (öre/kWh at full scale) on grid-funded charging, scaled by how much of
# the battery's headroom the remaining forecast solar could fill anyway (0 disables).
#SOLINTEG_SOLAR_RISK_PREMIUM_ORE=20
# How far ahead the solar risk premium counts forecast solar (hours). The premium scales with
# `futureSolar / batteryHeadroom` capped at 1; summed over the whole (up to 3-day) horizon that
# ratio saturates and the premium degenerates into a flat 20 ore/kWh tax on every grid-funded
# charge from March to October. 12 h covers the rest of a daylight day, which is the window over
# which solar can actually make a charge redundant. See OPTIMIZER-AUDIT.md P1-4.
#SOLINTEG_SOLAR_RISK_LOOKAHEAD_H=12
# Upstream risk models available for evaluation; both OFF here until local replay validates them.
#SOLINTEG_SOLAR_RISK_SURVIVAL=1
#SOLINTEG_DEFERRAL_UNTIL_NEEDED=1
# Annual battery-to-grid energy-tax refund (Skatteverket 739); OFF unless eligibility, filing
# and the ACTUAL regional tax paid are confirmed. Never assume the national 36-öre rate here.
#SOLINTEG_REFUND_CREDIT=1
#SOLINTEG_REFUND_TAX_PAID_ORE=0

# ── Planner hold (opt-in, shadow-only on this installation) ──
# Upstream's EMS-at-0W soft hold was verified on a different inverter. Our 50207/50208/50209
# full-freeze probe instead imported 1.3 kW and charged the battery. These are DIFFERENT
# recipes; neither soft hold nor solar-surplus hold is proven safe here. The local dispatcher
# keeps planner `hold` separate from executor `freeze` and never routes the former to 0 W EMS.
# Keep DISPATCH_FREEZE_HOLDS=0. Do not enable without a new physical probe and review.
#SOLINTEG_HOLD_ENABLED=1
# Gate 2: minimum gross PV (kW) for a slot to be hold-eligible.
#SOLINTEG_HOLD_MIN_PV_KW=3
# Gates 1+3: look-ahead window (hours) for the price-drop test and the refill guarantee.
#SOLINTEG_HOLD_REFILL_WINDOW_H=12
# Gate 3: refillable solar must cover remaining headroom × this margin (1.1 = full +10 %).
#SOLINTEG_HOLD_REFILL_MARGIN=1.1
# Gate 1: sell price now must beat the cheapest buy in the window by this many öre (0 = any
# non-negative spread).
#SOLINTEG_HOLD_PRICE_DROP_ORE=0

# ── Shared data file: poller writes it, the web app reads it ──
INVERTER_DATA_PATH=/opt/solinteg/live.json

# ── Telemetry time-series (SQLite). Inverter poller appends readings; web app appends
#    price curves + optimizer runs; weather poller appends irradiance/temp/wind.
#    All write the same file (WAL mode). Web-app telemetry is OFF unless this is set. ──
TELEMETRY_DB_PATH=/opt/solinteg/telemetry.db

# ── Ecowitt weather station (cloud API v3). Powers the solar-forecast calibration.
#    Keys from ecowitt.net → user profile → API keys. ──
ECOWITT_APPLICATION_KEY=your_application_key
ECOWITT_API_KEY=your_api_key
# Your station's MAC, from ecowitt.net device list.
ECOWITT_MAC=AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
WEATHER_POLL_INTERVAL=60

# ── Pricing / site config (web app; see lib/constants.ts). All optional — most defaults below
#    are the reference deployment's own measured contract terms (surcharge, VAT, skatt, export
#    bonus); GPS coordinates and battery replacement cost are illustrative placeholders, not
#    that installation's real numbers. Override all of these for your own contract/site;
#    PRICE_ZONE is the one most other Swedish (SE1-SE4) adopters would actually need to change.
#
#    IMPORTANT — unlike SOLINTEG_HOST/TELEMETRY_DB_PATH etc., changing one of THESE and just
#    restarting solinteg-web is NOT enough: fetchPrices()/fetchSolarForecast() use Next's
#    'use cache' + Partial Prerendering, so their result gets baked into the build output at
#    `npm run build` time, not read fresh per-request. deploy/to-nuc.sh's remote build step
#    now sources this file before building (2026-07-10 fix — it didn't before, so a build could
#    silently use stale/default values even though the running service had the override) so a
#    normal redeploy picks these up correctly; it only bites if you edit this file directly on
#    the NUC and just `systemctl restart solinteg-web` without rebuilding. ──
# SE1, SE2, SE3, or SE4 — Sundsvall uses SE2; falls back to SE2 if unset/invalid.
#PRICE_ZONE=SE2
# Your supplier's own påslag+elcertifikat, öre/kWh.
#SUPPLIER_SURCHARGE_ORE=11.5
# Your grid company's nätnytta export compensation, öre/kWh.
#EXPORT_BONUS_ORE=5.10
# Flat energiskatt + överföringsavgift, öre/kWh (buy-side only).
#SKATT_OVERFORING_ORE=71
# Moms on the energy price.
#VAT_RATE=0.25
# Decimal degrees — feeds the solar/weather forecast APIs.
#SITE_LATITUDE=57.64
#SITE_LONGITUDE=11.78
# Premium in öre/kWh applied only to forecast prices during optimization; 0 disables it.
#SOLINTEG_FORECAST_RISK_PREMIUM_ORE=10
#SOLAR_FORECAST_BIAS_FACTOR=1.75
# Open-Meteo `models=` value for the solar forecast, or empty string for the default
# best_match blend. metno_nordic (the default here) is Nordic-only (NO/SE/DK/FI) —
# outside that region, backtest your own candidates first with
# scripts/tools/compare-metno-solar.mjs against your own station history. See
# lib/constants.ts's SOLAR_FORECAST_MODEL comment for the full reasoning.
#SOLAR_FORECAST_MODEL=metno_nordic
# What you paid for inverter+battery — feeds the wear-cost model.
#BATTERY_REPLACEMENT_COST_SEK=60000

# ── Web app ──
# LAN-only deployment: no Clerk / no auth. The dashboard is open on the local network.

# ── Alerting (solinteg-watchdog.timer / solinteg-healthcheck.timer). See deploy/README.md
#    for the ntfy app + healthchecks.io setup steps (both are one-time, done in their own
#    apps/websites, not here). ──
NTFY_SERVER=https://ntfy.sh
# Pick a long random topic name — on the public ntfy.sh instance, anyone who knows/guesses
# it can read your alerts. Generate one with:
#   python -c "import secrets,base64; print('solinteg-' + base64.b32encode(secrets.token_bytes(15)).decode().rstrip('=').lower())"
NTFY_TOPIC=

# Quiet hours: hold back any push below NTFY_QUIET_HOURS_MIN_PRIORITY (default 5 = urgent) until
# NTFY_QUIET_HOURS_END, via ntfy's own delayed-delivery scheduling — the phone still gets it,
# just not at 3am. Most of what this file sends is informational (a stale weather source, a slow
# probe), and there's no reason for that to buzz as insistently as a real fault. Set START == END
# to disable quiet hours entirely. The code default (notify.py, if these stay commented out) is
# 22:00-07:00; shown here as 21:00-07:00, the reference deployment's own preferred window — pick
# whatever suits your own sleep schedule.
#NTFY_QUIET_HOURS_START=21
#NTFY_QUIET_HOURS_END=7
# Only PRIORITY_URGENT (5) bypasses by default — reserved for the one case that can mean a write
# landed on the inverter and wasn't confirmed reverted (see healthcheck.py's check_control_errors
# and watchdog.py/heating_watchdog.py's own urgent sends). Lower it to 4 if you also want
# PRIORITY_HIGH (a dead poller, a full disk, a failed backup — real, but not a 3am problem) to
# wake you immediately instead of waiting.
#NTFY_QUIET_HOURS_MIN_PRIORITY=5

# Optional: a healthchecks.io "ping URL" (free tier). Pinged every 10 min by
# solinteg-heartbeat-ping.timer; healthchecks.io itself alerts (via its own ntfy/email/
# webhook integration) if the ping ever stops arriving — the one failure mode nothing
# running ON the NUC can detect (the NUC being fully off). Leave blank to skip this layer.
#HEALTHCHECKS_PING_URL=https://hc-ping.com/your-uuid-here

# Optional tuning for the alerting layer — sane defaults, only touch after checking real
# behaviour (query control_actions/readings for the actual timing/staleness distribution):
#WATCHDOG_STALE_S=240
#WATCHDOG_ALERT_COOLDOWN_S=1800
#POLLER_STALE_S=300
#WEATHER_STALE_S=1800
#CONTROL_ERROR_WINDOW_S=900
#HEALTHCHECK_ALERT_COOLDOWN_S=14400
# How long an issue must stay ABSENT before the healthcheck declares it resolved. Keep it well
# above the recurrence gap of a flapping fault: resolving too eagerly deletes the dedup state, so
# every recurrence reads as a brand-new issue and the cooldown above never engages — which turns
# an intermittent hardware fault into a stream of alert/resolved pairs.
#HEALTHCHECK_RESOLVE_QUIET_S=10800
# Active liveness probe for the SECOND weather source (MET Norway'''s Thredds server, tier 2 of
# the solar/temperature chain, only present when SOLAR_FORECAST_MODEL=metno_nordic). Everything
# else the healthcheck looks at is telemetry something already produced; this one has to make its
# own signal, because tier 2 only ever runs when Open-Meteo has already failed and can therefore
# sit dead for weeks without anything noticing. Daily, not tighter: each probe makes
# thredds.met.no subset a live multi-GB NetCDF server-side. 0 disables it — do that if you are
# not on metno_nordic, since the tier does not exist then.
#WEATHER_FALLBACK_PROBE_INTERVAL_H=24
# Faster re-probe cadence while the LAST probe failed, so a transient met.no timeout (its normal
# case, not something to treat as a real outage on the first miss) gets re-tested — and can clear
# itself — well before the next HEALTHCHECK_ALERT_COOLDOWN_S push repeats a verdict nobody has
# re-checked in a day.
#WEATHER_FALLBACK_PROBE_RETRY_INTERVAL_H=2
#WEATHER_FALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/weather-fallback-check
#WEATHER_FALLBACK_TIMEOUT_S=120
# Tier-3 analogue of the above: how far solar-forecast-cache.json's fetchedAt may lag a run
# that just used a live forecast before the WRITE path (not just the fallback chain) counts as
# broken. Passive, unlike the tier-2 probe (no network call to make; the file is rewritten as a
# side effect of normal traffic, so this just checks the output is what healthy traffic implies).
#SOLAR_CACHE_WRITE_LAG_MAX_S=7200
#DISPATCH_SOC_DIVERGENCE_KWH=3.0

# Live-load discharge tracking (see dispatch_loop.py's module docstring, SAFETY section): during
# a forced discharge, corrects the plan's fixed forecast-based power against live pv_w/
# house_load_w so a load spike (or a solar miss) is absorbed by the battery instead of leaking
# to/from the grid at full price. "0" reverts discharge slots to the plan's fixed power, same
# as before this existed — a kill switch, not a tuning knob.
#DISPATCH_LIVE_LOAD_TRACKING=1
# The same correction on CHARGE slots, which previously had only the binary solar-funding veto
# (the plan's full power, or Auto). Live solar above forecast then exported instead of charging,
# and live load above forecast landed on top of the commanded charge at the meter.
# "shadow" (default) computes and logs the target it WOULD command without applying it; "1" applies
# it; "0" disables it. Review a few days of control_actions rows (detail_json.tracked_charge_w vs
# planned_power_w) before arming. See OPTIMIZER-AUDIT.md E2.
#DISPATCH_LIVE_LOAD_TRACKING_CHARGE=shadow

# Realise a planned hold as a REAL battery freeze (50207/50208/50209 = 0 in EMS BattCtrl) instead of
# the ~220 W battery-save emulation. The emulation works but still moves ~0.055 kWh/slot out of the
# battery at the CURRENT price when the point of the hold is to keep it for a dearer one -- ~2.6 kWh
# over a 12 h overnight hold.
# OFF by default, and do not turn it on without running scripts/tools/probe_freeze.py AT NIGHT on
# your own unit first. The failure shape is nasty: MODBUS.md confirms that a restrictive cap written
# before mode entry is STORED BUT NEVER ENFORCED, so a naive freeze reads back as frozen while the
# battery keeps draining. inverter_control.freeze_battery writes in the verified order and then
# verifies the battery physically stopped, but whether this firmware honours it at all is exactly
# what the probe is for. Also unverified: whether the inverter's own SoC floor can still act with AC
# input blocked -- so a freeze is refused below SOC_FLOOR_PCT + 5. See OPTIMIZER-AUDIT.md E6.
#DISPATCH_FREEZE_HOLDS=0
# Live-tracked discharge target is rounded to the nearest this many watts.
#DISPATCH_LIVE_LOAD_ROUND_W=100
# Same-slot same-action retargets smaller than this (W) wait for the next REASSERT_S write
# instead of writing the register every tick — ordinary load noise buys nothing; a genuinely
# large change (heat pump scale) clears this immediately. Must be > the ROUND_W above.
#DISPATCH_LIVE_LOAD_DEADBAND_W=250
# Empirical battery-save drift used by the optimizer and executor during qualified planned hold
# The forecast value is the observed physical drain (~220 W), not the inverter command target.
# SOLINTEG_BATTERY_SAVE_FORECAST_DRAIN_W=220
# slots. The value is the inverter-side discharge target in watts, rounded to 10 W. Set to 0 to
# disable. SOLINTEG_BATTERY_SAVE_DRIFT_W takes precedence; DISPATCH_BATTERY_SAVE_W is retained
# as a legacy fallback.
#SOLINTEG_BATTERY_SAVE_DRIFT_W=220

# Safety recovery overrides ALL optimizer actions, including battery-save and forced discharge.
# Defaults: begins below SOLINTEG_SOC_FLOOR_PCT + 0.5 kWh, charges at least 300 W,
# and continues until SOLINTEG_SOC_FLOOR_PCT + 8 percentage points.
# These defaults are NOT Sunwoda BMS thresholds. They may trigger repeated grid-funded
# recovery when the normal optimizer is allowed to discharge below the entry band.
# Sundsvall trial (read-only BMS verification 2026-10-23): hardware min SoC 10%,
# BMS allows discharge at 15%; keep software floor 12% and instead use entry ~13%,
# exit 16%, retaining 300 W emergency charging. See SOC-RECOVERY-2026-10-23.md.
# These two site-tuning values are OPT-IN, not cross-install defaults:
#DISPATCH_SOC_FLOOR_GUARD_HEADROOM_KWH=0.15
#DISPATCH_SOC_FLOOR_RECOVERY_W=300
#DISPATCH_SOC_RECOVERY_EXIT_PCT=21
# On a site using SOLINTEG_PLANNER_RESERVE_PCT=20, recovery starts below
# 19.5% and continues to at least 21%. The 12% physical floor is separate.
# Site overrides must be reconciled with both the optimizer and dispatch.
#SOLINTEG_PLANNER_RESERVE_PCT=20
# Reserve-hold is a SEPARATE experimental open-cap 0 W function. Do not enable
# without physical verification on this inverter and explicit operator approval;
# a planning reserve by itself must NOT activate this mode.
#DISPATCH_RESERVE_HOLD_VERIFIED=0
#
# Two independent L3 ECONOMIC controls (not fuse/overcurrent protection):
# Battery coordination reduces house battery discharge during EV-like L3 load.
# Mode 0 (default) = off; shadow = diagnostics only; 1 = battery adjustments
# after independent validation and explicit approval.
#DISPATCH_L3_BATTERY_MODE=0
# Charge-side guard estimates L3 headroom using fresh phase CT values and
# poller's read-only 50006=0 balance read on the EXISTING Modbus connection.
# shadow (default) only logs; 1 caps high-power charge if verified.
# Missing or inconsistent data in mode 1 limits high charging to the SoC
# emergency recovery baseline (300 W by default). EV/EVCC remains unchanged.
#DISPATCH_L3_CHARGE_GUARD_MODE=shadow
# Shared economic L3 budget in W for the two controls (range 300..4600).
#DISPATCH_L3_CHARGE_BUDGET_W=4000

# ── EVCC solar + economic shadow (NOT enabled or deployed by these examples) ──
# Reuses the published house optimizer's full buy/sell price curve and forecast
# risk premium. Without a trusted, fresh HA EV session file, NO grid top-up is
# suggested; solar-only shadow behaviour is retained.
#EV_ENABLED=0
#EVCC_MAX_CURRENT_A=6
#EV_SESSION_PATH=/opt/solinteg/volvo-energy-session.json
#
# Independent read-only HA REST bridge (deploy/solinteg-ev-ha.service, OFF by default).
# HA_API_URL must be an authenticated, TLS-validated HTTPS endpoint or a
# private RFC1918 cluster IP (example shown only, resolve the current service IP).
#HA_API_URL=http://10.0.0.20:8123
#HA_READONLY_TOKEN_FILE=/opt/solinteg/ha-readonly.token
#EV_STATUS_PATH=/opt/solinteg/ev-session-status.json
#EV_SETTINGS_PATH=/opt/solinteg/ev-settings.json
#EV_HA_POLL_SECONDS=60
#
# The HA bridge generates two SEPARATE local records:
# - EV_SESSION_PATH: always INVALID for physical/control use. OEM updatedAt
#   is last change, not a BMS sample timestamp; no source-verified controls.
# - EV_SHADOW_SESSION_PATH: purely advisory economics using an explicit,
#   UNVERIFIED capacity and charging-loss SCENARIO if opted into below.
#EV_SHADOW_SESSION_PATH=/opt/solinteg/ev-shadow-session.json
#EV_SHADOW_ASSUMPTIONS_ENABLED=0
#EV_SHADOW_ASSUMED_USABLE_KWH=
#EV_SHADOW_ASSUMED_AC_EFFICIENCY=
#
# The independent price planner NEVER creates an EVCC/network connection or
# controls the house battery; EV_SHADOW_PLANNER_ENABLED is OFF by default.
# It can run without a Waveshare connection. Writes only EV_SHADOW_PLAN_PATH.
#EV_SHADOW_PLANNER_ENABLED=0
#EV_SHADOW_PLAN_PATH=/opt/solinteg/ev-shadow-plan.json
#EV_SHADOW_POLL_SECONDS=60
#EV_SHADOW_MAX_CURRENT_A=6
#
# These production-grade values need independent calibration AND verified
# per-phase and EVCC fail-safes. The bridge does not currently promote any
# OEM observation into the strict session, even when these are configured.
#EV_USABLE_BATTERY_KWH=
#EV_AC_CHARGE_EFFICIENCY=
#
# The Solinteg /ev web UI is shadow-only; changing settings never sends
# commands to Volvo/EVCC/inverter. Writes to preferences are denied unless
# BOTH an explicit opt-in and a server-only random 32+ char token are provided.
# No live settings API enablement in this template.
#EV_SHADOW_SETTINGS_WRITES_ENABLED=0
#EV_SHADOW_SETTINGS_WRITE_TOKEN=
#
# Session JSON (must be written by a trusted, fresh HA reader):
#   "charge_mode": "deadline" => reach 80% by fixed "departure_at_utc"
#   "charge_mode": "economy"  => NO departure, price-only until 80%
#   "max_buy_ore_per_kwh": optional absolute full retail price ceiling in economy
# Default when the field is missing: deadline (backward compatibility).
# Without a verified session and independent phase safety, EVCC writes remain OFF.
# The EVCC link/physical commands require separate operator-only validation;
# do NOT set EVCC_CONTROL_ARMED or EVCC_MANUAL_TEST in this env file.

# Replan triggers (see dispatch_loop.py's module docstring, "REPLAN TRIGGERS" section): lets
# the dispatch loop ask the web app to compute a fresh plan on drift/guard/missing-plan
# conditions instead of only ever picking one up from the next timer-driven render (see
# deploy/solinteg-telemetry.timer). "0" = off (kill switch, reverts to the pre-feature
# timer-only cadence); "1" = on (default); "shadow" = compute the same decision and log it,
# but never actually call out — use this to watch trigger frequency in journalctl before
# letting it touch the web app.
#DISPATCH_REPLAN_TRIGGERS=1
# Preemptive SoC-drift threshold (kWh) for requesting a replan — deliberately HALF of
# DISPATCH_SOC_DIVERGENCE_KWH's own default above, so a fresh, live-anchored plan usually
# already exists by the time drift would otherwise cross that guard's own wider threshold.
#DISPATCH_REPLAN_DRIFT_KWH=1.5
# Floor between replan requests, regardless of which (or how many) trigger conditions fired
# in that window — coalesces bursty triggers behind one request instead of one each.
#DISPATCH_REPLAN_DEBOUNCE_S=120
# Where to POST the replan request — the web app runs on the same NUC by default.
#DISPATCH_REPLAN_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/replan
#DISPATCH_SOLAR_SHORTFALL_KWH=0.5

# ── Site-level (utility-meter) import limit — the fuse-level backstop.
#    Written ONCE at dispatch-loop startup to registers 50007/50009, in watts. Unset/0 = never
#    written, which is the default.
#    Why you might want it: during a forced charge the meter sees `battery charge + house load`.
#    clamp_power_w bounds only the battery's own power, and the optimizer's grid-cap check uses a
#    FORECAST load — a load spike above forecast lands on top of the commanded charge. 50208/50209
#    are no help: they cap the inverter's own AC exchange, which excludes house load (MODBUS.md).
#    Why it is off by default: these two registers come from a third-party reverse-engineering doc,
#    not from an on-device probe here. Run `python3 scripts/tools/probe_import_limit.py` on YOUR
#    unit first — the failure mode to expect is "stored but not enforced", which is exactly what
#    50209=0 turned out to do when written pre-mode.
#    Set it to your real main-fuse rating (3x16A ~ 11000, 3x20A ~ 13800, 3x25A ~ 17300).
#DISPATCH_IMPORT_LIMIT_W=11000
#DISK_FREE_MIN_PCT=10
#BACKUP_KEEP=21

# ── Offsite backup mirror (scripts/services/backup_offsite.py / solinteg-backup-offsite.timer).
#    Local nightly backups only protect against DB corruption or a bad deploy, not a dead disk
#    or a dead NUC — this mirrors BACKUP_DIR to a cloud remote via rclone. Requires `rclone`
#    installed (apt install rclone) — see deploy/README.md §12d. Leave RCLONE_OFFSITE_DEST blank
#    to skip this layer entirely (the local-only backup still runs). No rclone config file is
#    used; credentials are supplied via RCLONE_CONFIG_<REMOTE>_* env vars below, matching the
#    remote name in RCLONE_OFFSITE_DEST. Example below is for Backblaze B2 (free tier, 10 GB) —
#    swap the RCLONE_CONFIG_B2_* values and remote name for a different backend if preferred. ──
#RCLONE_OFFSITE_DEST=b2:your-bucket-name
#RCLONE_CONFIG_B2_TYPE=b2
#RCLONE_CONFIG_B2_ACCOUNT=your_application_key_id
#RCLONE_CONFIG_B2_KEY=your_application_key
